Q(TAG_RC, "Chlamydia trachomatis: inclusion body culture",
  "A research lab inoculates McCoy cell monolayers with an endocervical specimen and examines the cells 48 hours later with iodine stain. Compact, glycogen-rich cytoplasmic inclusions are seen adjacent to the nucleus. What does this technique detect?",
  ["Growth of the organism inside a cultured eukaryotic cell line",
   "Direct binding of a fluorescent antibody to a swab smear",
   "Amplification of a conserved ribosomal DNA sequence",
   "Formation of a visible precipitin ring in agar gel",
   "A color change from fermentation of a carbohydrate substrate"],
  "Chlamydia trachomatis cannot be grown on artificial media because it is an obligate intracellular organism, so cell culture (historically McCoy or HeLa cells) was the reference method; the intracytoplasmic inclusion contains glycogen and stains with iodine. Cell culture is slow and insensitive compared with nucleic acid amplification testing, which has replaced it for routine diagnosis and is now the clinical standard.",
  difficulty="medium",
  why={"Direct binding of a fluorescent antibody": "Direct fluorescent antibody staining is a separate, faster method applied straight to a smear, not to cultured monolayers.",
       "Amplification of a conserved ribosomal": "That describes nucleic acid amplification testing, which does not require live cell culture.",
       "Formation of a visible precipitin ring": "Precipitin rings are used in older serologic assays, not in monolayer culture.",
       "A color change from fermentation": "Chlamydia has no fermentative metabolism to detect this way."}),

