Q(TAG_RC, "Chlamydia trachomatis: specimen sensitivity",
  "A clinic wants to maximize detection of Chlamydia trachomatis in asymptomatic women being screened during a routine visit. Which specimen type gives the highest sensitivity on nucleic acid amplification testing?",
  ["A self-collected or clinician-collected vaginal swab",
   "A first-void urine sample collected after voiding twice",
   "An endocervical swab placed in bacterial transport broth",
   "A blood sample tested for organism-specific IgM antibody",
   "A rectal swab regardless of the site of exposure"],
  "Vaginal swabs, whether self- or clinician-collected, yield the highest NAAT sensitivity for chlamydia in women, equal to or better than endocervical swabs, and are more sensitive than urine, which performs better in men than in women. Serology is not used to diagnose acute urogenital infection.",
  difficulty="medium",
  why={"A first-void urine sample collected after voiding": "Urine testing has somewhat lower sensitivity than a vaginal swab in women and requires first-void, not post-void, collection.",
       "An endocervical swab placed in bacterial transport": "Endocervical swabs work but are not more sensitive than vaginal swabs and require a speculum exam.",
       "A blood sample tested for organism-specific IgM": "Serology reflects past exposure and is not used to diagnose current urogenital infection.",
       "A rectal swab regardless of the site of exposure": "Rectal swabs are appropriate only when rectal exposure or symptoms are present, not as a general screening specimen."}),

Q(TAG_RC, "Chlamydophila pneumoniae: biphasic illness",
  "A 19-year-old college student develops hoarseness and sore throat that improve over a week, followed by a slowly worsening dry cough, low-grade fever, and patchy infiltrates on chest x-ray. Several dormmates have had a similar illness. Which organism best fits this two-stage course?",
  ["Chlamydophila pneumoniae",
   "Chlamydia psittaci",
   "Coxiella burnetii",
   "Legionella pneumophila",
   "Streptococcus pneumoniae"],
  "Chlamydophila (Chlamydia) pneumoniae classically causes a biphasic illness in young adults living in close quarters: pharyngitis or laryngitis first, followed one to two weeks later by a mild, slowly progressive atypical pneumonia. It spreads person to person by respiratory droplets, unlike the animal-exposure organisms in this group.",
  difficulty="medium",
  why={"Chlamydia psittaci": "Psittacosis follows bird exposure rather than dormmate contact and lacks the classic two-stage pharyngitis-then-pneumonia course.",
       "Coxiella burnetii": "Q fever follows exposure to parturient livestock, not person-to-person spread among students.",
       "Legionella pneumophila": "Legionella is linked to contaminated water sources, not close-quarters person-to-person spread.",
       "Streptococcus pneumoniae": "Pneumococcal pneumonia presents acutely with high fever and lobar consolidation, not a gradual biphasic course."}),

Q(TAG_RC, "Chlamydia: virulence and phagolysosome evasion",
  "After a Chlamydia trachomatis elementary body is endocytosed by a genital epithelial cell, the organism survives and converts into its replicating form inside a membrane-bound vacuole. What allows this vacuole to avoid destruction?",
  ["Secreted inclusion membrane proteins block fusion with lysosomes",
   "A thick outer capsule shields the vacuole from acidification",
   "Released catalase neutralizes lysosomal reactive oxygen species",
   "The organism escapes into the cytoplasm before lysosomes arrive",
   "Host actin polymerization walls off the lysosome permanently"],
  "Chlamydia uses a type III secretion system to insert bacterial (Inc) proteins into the inclusion membrane, which modifies vesicle trafficking so the inclusion does not fuse with lysosomes, letting the reticulate body replicate safely inside the cell until it converts back to infectious elementary bodies.",
  difficulty="hard",
  why={"A thick outer capsule shields the vacuole": "Chlamydia lacks a polysaccharide capsule of this kind.",
       "Released catalase neutralizes lysosomal": "Catalase-based oxidant defense is a staphylococcal strategy, not the chlamydial inclusion mechanism.",
       "The organism escapes into the cytoplasm": "Chlamydia remains within its membrane-bound inclusion throughout its replicative cycle.",
       "Host actin polymerization walls off the lysosome": "Actin-based motility mechanisms are used by organisms such as Listeria and Shigella, not by Chlamydia."}),

