Q(TAG_MOB, "Gardnerella vaginalis: Amsel criteria",
  "A 29-year-old woman has a thin, gray vaginal discharge. Testing shows a vaginal pH of 5.2, a fishy odor when potassium hydroxide is added, and epithelial cells with borders obscured by adherent coccobacilli. Only three of the four standard criteria are met. Which additional finding would complete the diagnosis?",
  ["Absence of pain, itching, or overt inflammation on examination",
   "Motile, flagellated organisms seen on the wet mount preparation",
   "A cervix that bleeds easily on contact with a swab",
   "Numerous white blood cells surrounding vaginal epithelial cells",
   "A strawberry-like appearance of the cervical surface"],
  "The Amsel criteria for bacterial vaginosis require three of four findings: thin homogeneous discharge, vaginal pH above 4.5, a positive whiff test, and clue cells; here the fourth criterion is the notable lack of accompanying inflammation, since bacterial vaginosis is a shift in flora rather than a true inflammatory infection, distinguishing it from trichomoniasis.",
  difficulty="medium",
  why={"Motile, flagellated organisms seen on the wet mount": "Motile flagellates on wet mount indicate Trichomonas vaginalis, not bacterial vaginosis.",
       "A cervix that bleeds easily on contact": "A friable, easily bleeding cervix suggests cervicitis from chlamydia or gonorrhea, not bacterial vaginosis.",
       "Numerous white blood cells surrounding vaginal": "Bacterial vaginosis is characterized by a paucity of white blood cells, unlike trichomoniasis or candidiasis.",
       "A strawberry-like appearance of the cervical": "A strawberry cervix is a classic sign of trichomonal infection."}),

Q(TAG_MOB, "Gardnerella vaginalis: recurrence management",
  "A 34-year-old woman has her fourth episode of bacterial vaginosis this year, each initially responding to oral metronidazole before symptoms return within two months. Her male partner has no symptoms. What is the most appropriate next step?",
  ["Consider a longer suppressive or maintenance antimicrobial regimen for her",
   "Treat her male partner empirically with the same oral antibiotic",
   "Switch to a single injection of intramuscular benzathine penicillin",
   "Begin daily oral fluconazole to prevent recurrent yeast overgrowth",
   "Recommend permanent discontinuation of any vaginal hygiene products alone"],
  "Recurrent bacterial vaginosis is managed by extending or repeating first-line therapy (oral or vaginal metronidazole or clindamycin) and, for frequent recurrence, using a longer maintenance course; treating male partners has not been shown to reduce recurrence, since bacterial vaginosis reflects a disturbed vaginal ecosystem rather than a classic sexually transmitted infection passed back and forth.",
  difficulty="medium",
  why={"Treat her male partner empirically with the same": "Partner treatment does not reduce recurrence rates in bacterial vaginosis and is not recommended.",
       "Switch to a single injection of intramuscular": "Benzathine penicillin has no role in bacterial vaginosis and does not target the causative anaerobes.",
       "Begin daily oral fluconazole to prevent recurrent": "Fluconazole is an antifungal and would not address bacterial, non-fungal overgrowth.",
       "Recommend permanent discontinuation of any vaginal hygiene": "While douching is a risk factor, stopping it alone does not reliably resolve established recurrent disease."}),

